"Devolution and Scotland | UK and Scottish governments (https://www.deliveringforscotland.gov.uk/scotland-in-the-uk/devolution/)\nciteturn17view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.deliveringforscotland.gov.uk/scotland-in-the-uk/devolution/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 96\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: [Input]\nL3: \nL4:   * 【1†Households】\nL5:   * 【2†Business】\nL6:   * 【3†Defence & security】\nL7:   * 【4†Levelling up】\nL8:   * 【5†Scotland in the UK】\nL9:   * 【6†Case studies】\nL10: \nL11: 【7†Deliveringfor Scotland】\nL12:   * 【1†Households】\nL13:     * 【8†Benefits entitlement】\nL14:     * 【9†UK National Minimum & Living Wage rates】\nL15:     * 【10†Pensions】\nL16:     * 【11†Help with childcare】\nL17:     * 【12†Help to Save】\nL18:     * 【13†Marriage allowance】\nL19:   * 【2†Business】\nL20:     * 【14†Start Up Loans】\nL21:     * 【15†Help with exporting】\nL22:     * 【16†Help to grow】\nL23:     * 【17†Manage your tax】\nL24:   * 【3†Defence & security】\nL25:     * 【18†The Royal Navy in Scotland】\nL26:     * 【19†The Army in Scotland】\nL27:     * 【20†The Royal Air Force in Scotland】\nL28:     * 【21†UK Defence: A Cornerstone of Scottish Growth】\nL29:     * 【22†Careers in the Ministry of Defence】\nL30:   * 【5†Scotland in the UK】\nL31:     * 【23†Public spending】\nL32:     * 【24†Global Influence】\nL33:     * 【25†Devolution】\nL34: 【7†Home】 » 【5†Scotland in the UK】 »\nL35: # Devolution\nL36: \nL37: Scotland has two governments, the UK and Scottish governments.  Each holds power and responsibility over different things and they work together for the people of Scotland.\nL38: \nL39: 【26†Scottish Devolution Timeline†www.youtube-nocookie.com】\nL40: ## What is devolution?\nL41: \nL42: Devolution is the decentralisation of power and accountability to be closer to citizens.  The end result should be that local factors are better represented in decision making.\nL43: \nL44: In 1999 the 【27†UK Government†gov.uk】 established a 【28†Scottish Parliament†www.parliament.scot】 and Scottish Executive with power and responsibility over the majority of public services in Scotland.  The Scottish Executive was later renamed the 【29†Scottish Government†www.gov.scot】.\nL45: Areas of UK Government responsibility are ‘reserved’, and areas of Scottish Government responsibility are ‘devolved’.\nL46: \nL47: The Scottish Parliament cannot make laws outside its devolved responsibilities.  There is also a convention that the UK Government will seek the consent of the Scottish Parliament if it is passing laws that could impact on devolved areas.\nL48: Devolved matters: Scottish Government  | Reserved matters: UK Government\nL49: --- | ---\nL50: Agriculture, forestry and fisheries  | Broadcasting\nL51: Education and training  | Constitution\nL52: Environment  | Defence and national security\nL53: Health, care and social services  | Immigration\nL54: Housing and land use planning  | Energy\nL55: Law and order  | Employment\nL56: Local government  | Equal opportunities\nL57: Sport, arts and tourism  | Foreign affairs and international relations\nL58: Parts of social security  | Macroeconomic and fiscal policy\nL59: Some forms of taxation  | Pensions, and parts of social security\nL60: Many aspects of transport  | Trade, including international trade\nL61: The two governments work together on many issues, supported by the 【30†Scotland Office†www.gov.uk】.\nL62: ## The history of devolution\nL63: \nL64: In September 1997 there was a referendum in Scotland in which people voted for devolution.  The 【31†UK Parliament†www.parliament.uk】 then passed the 【32†Scotland Act 1998†www.legislation.gov.uk】 which established the Scottish Parliament, which opened in 1999.\nL65: Devolution has not stood still since 1999, with a number of changes and additions to the Scottish Parliament’s powers.  These include the 【33†Scotland Act 2016†www.legislation.gov.uk】 and 【34†Scotland Act 2012†www.legislation.gov.uk】, which each devolved significant further powers with cross-party agreement.\nL66: ## How is the Scottish Government funded?\nL67: \nL68: The Scottish Government is responsible for managing its own expenditure and is accountable to the Scottish Parliament.  The Scottish Government’s funding comes from a combination of devolved taxes and revenues as well as the Block Grant.\nL69: The Block Grant is the funding transferred to the Scottish Government from the UK Government.  The Block Grant is paid for from taxes and revenues collected across the whole of the UK.  This ensures there is steady and secure funding for the Scottish Government.  The devolution settlement means that expenditure per person on public services in Scotland is higher than the UK average.\nL70: \nL71: 【23†Read more about public spending in Scotland】\nL72: ## Devolution and Social Security\nL73: \nL74: Responsibility for delivering some benefits in Scotland is being devolved to the Scottish Government.  The new 【35†Scottish social security†www.gov.scot】 agency will have responsibility for:\nL75:   * Ill Health and Disability Benefits:\nL76:     * Disability Living Allowance\nL77:     * Personal Independence Payment\nL78:     * Attendance Allowance\nL79:     * Severe Disablement Allowance\nL80:     * Industrial Injuries Disablement Benefit\nL81:   * Carers Allowance\nL82:   * Sure Start Maternity Grant (replaced by the Best Start Grant)\nL83:   * Funeral Expenses\nL84:   * Winter Heating Payment and Pension Age Winter Heating Payment\nL85:   * Discretionary Housing Payments\nL86:   * Some powers in relation to Universal Credit (e.g. the ability to split payments between household members)\nL87: ## More information\nL88: \nL89: Find out more about 【5†Scotland in the UK】.\nL90: \nL91:   * 【36†About us】\nL92:   * 【37†Site map】\nL93:   * 【38†Terms & conditions】\nL94:   * 【39†Privacy & cookies policy】\nL95:   * 【40†Accessibility statement】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhat is the Rule of Law? | World Justice Project (https://worldjusticeproject.org/about-us/overview/what-rule-law)\nciteturn17view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://worldjusticeproject.org/about-us/overview/what-rule-law\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 149\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: The rule of law is the foundation for healthy communities of justice, opportunity, and peace.\nL3: \nL4: WILLIAM H. NEUKOM\nL5: \nL6: CO-FOUNDER & CEO, WORLD JUSTICE PROJECT\nL7: \nL8: 【1†Image: A group of people holding hands】\nL9: ## Our Definition\nL10: \nL11: The idea of rule of law, traceable to ancient scholars, resonates in most major legal traditions. WJP’s definition of rule of law is built around four universal principles, developed in accordance with internationally accepted standards and norms, in consultation with a wide variety of experts worldwide.\nL12: \nL13: \nL14: \nL15:   * 【2†ENGLISH】\nL16:   * 【3†ESPAÑOL】\nL17:   * 【4†FRANÇAIS】\nL18:   * 【5†عربي】\nL19:   * 【6†中国人】\nL20:   * 【7†РУССКИЙ】\nL21: ## rule · of · law\nL22: \nL23: The rule of law is a durable system of laws, institutions, norms, and community commitment that delivers four universal principles: accountability, just law, open government, and accessible and impartial justice.\nL24:   * Accountability The government as well as private actors are accountable under the law.\nL25:   * Just Law The law is clear, publicized, and stable and is applied evenly. It ensures human rights as well as property, contract, and procedural rights.\nL26:   * Open Government The processes by which the law is adopted, administered, adjudicated, and enforced are accessible, fair, and efficient.\nL27:   * Accessible and Impartial Justice Justice is delivered timely by competent, ethical, and independent representatives and neutrals who are accessible, have adequate resources, and reflect the makeup of the communities they serve.\nL28: ## estado · de · derecho\nL29: \nL30: El Estado de Derecho es un sistema de leyes, instituciones, normas y compromiso comunitario, en el cual se cumplen cuatro principios universales: rendición de cuentas, leyes justas, gobierno abierto y justicia accesible e imparcial.\nL31:   * Rendición de cuentas Tanto el gobierno como los agentes privados rinden cuentas ante la ley.\nL32:   * Leyes justas La ley es clara, publicitada y estable, y se aplica de manera uniforme. Garantiza los derechos humanos, así como los derechos de propiedad, contractuales y procesales.\nL33:   * Gobierno Abierto Los procesos mediante los cuales la ley se adopta, administra, juzga y se hace cumplir son accesibles, justos y eficientes.\nL34:   * Justicia accesible e imparcial La justicia se imparte oportunamente por representantes independientes y neutrales, los cuales son accesibles, tienen recursos adecuados y reflejan la composición de las comunidades a las que sirven.\nL35: ## état · de · droit\nL36: \nL37: L’État de droit est un système pérenne de lois, d'institutions, de normes et d'engagement communautaire qui respecte quatre principes universels: la responsabilité, une loi juste, un gouvernement ouvert et une justice accessible et impartiale.\nL38:   * Responsabilité Le gouvernement ainsi que les acteurs privés sont responsables devant la loi.\nL39:   * Loi Juste La loi est claire, médiatisée, stable et appliquée uniformément. Elle garantit les droits de l'homme ainsi que les droits de propriété, contractuels et procéduraux.\nL40:   * Gouvernement Ouvert Les processus par lesquels la loi est adoptée, administrée, jugée et appliquée sont accessibles, équitables et efficaces.\nL41:   * Justice Accessible et Impartiale La justice est rendue dans les délais par des représentants et des neutres compétents, éthiques et indépendants qui sont accessibles, disposent de ressources adéquates et reflètent la composition des communautés qu'ils desservent.\nL42: ## سيادة القانون\nL43: \nL44: منظومة متكاملة ومستدامة تتكون من القوانين، والمؤسسات، والقيم، والالتزام المجتمعي، تهدف إلى تحقيق أربعة مبادئ أساسية؛ وهي المساءلة، والقانون العادل، والحكومة المفتوحة، والعدالة النزيهة المحايدة.\nL45:   * المساءلة تعني خضوع الجميع للمساءلة بموجب القانون؛ متضمنة الحكومة والجهات المستقلة والفاعلة.\nL46:   * القانون العادل يعني أن القوانين يجب أن تكون معلنة، وواضحة، ومنصفة، وثابتة، وأن تُطبق بالتساوي على الجميع دون تحيز؛ بما يكفل حقوق الإنسان، وحقوق الملكية، والعقود، وكذا الحقوق الإجرائية.\nL47:   * الحكومة المفتوحة هي الإجراءات التي من خلالها تُبنى القوانين وتدار وتنفذ، ويفصل فيها، ويجب أن تكون شفافة، ومتاحة، وعادلة، وفعالة.\nL48:   * العدالة النزيهة والمحايدة تتحقق العدالة في الوقت المناسب من قبل ممثلين ووسطاء وأخلاقيين مختصين ومستقلين، يتمتعون بالحيادية للقيام بمهامهم، ولديهم الموارد الكافية لتقديم العدالة بشكل فعال، ويعكسون تركيبة المجتمعات التي يخدمونها.\nL49: ## 法治\nL50: \nL51: 法治是一个由法律、机构、规范和社区承诺构成的持久体系，它体现了四项普遍原则：责任制、公正的法律、开放的政府，以及易于获得的公正司法。\nL52: \nL53:   * 责任制 政府和私人行为方均须依法承担责任。\nL54:   * 公正的法律 法律明确、公开、稳定且公平适用，确保人权以及财产权、合同权利和程序性权利。\nL55:   * 开放的政府 法律的立法、治理、裁决和执行的过程是易于理解、公平及高效的。\nL56:   * 易于获得的公正司法 由有能力、有道德的独立代表和中立方及时实现司法公正，他们平易近人，拥有充足的资源，并体现了其所服务社区的构成。\nL57: ## Верховенство · права\nL58: \nL59: The это устойчивая система, включающая законодательство, государственные и общественные институты, принципы и правила общежития. ç Она обеспечивает реализацию четырех универсальных принципов: ответственности, справедливости законов, открытости управление, доступности и беспристрастности правосудия.\nL60:   * Ответственность TВсе должностные лица, государственные органы, а также частные лица несут отвественность на основании закона.\nL61:   * Cправедливость законa Справедливым называется тот закон, который является ясным и понятным, он должем быть опубликован и доступен для всех. Такой закон не должен подвергаться постоянным поправкам, и должен применяться одинаково ко всем. Справедливый закон гарантирует права человека, права собственности, договорные и процессуальные права.\nL62:   * Открытость управления Процедуры, в рамках которых законы принимаются, исполняются и изменяются, являются доступными, справедливыми и эффективными.\nL63:   * Доступность и беспристрастность правосудия Jпредполагает, что правосудие осуществляется своевременно компетентными, этичными и независимыми представителями и судьями, которые доступны, располагают соответствующими ресурсами и отражают состав населения, с которым они работают.\nL64: ## Understanding the universal principles\nL65: \nL66: The four universal principles are building blocks for any rule of law system. To demonstrate the impact of each principle in practice, we have developed a set of illustrative questions–by no means comprehensive–for you to explore.\nL67: \nL68: [Input]\nL69: ### Accountability\nL70: \nL71: Click to view Questions\nL72: \nL73: Accountability\nL74: Illustrative questions encompassed in this principle\nL75: \nL76:   * Are politicians and government officials held accountable for corruption?\nL77:   * Do people face legal consequences for wrongdoing regardless of their wealth or status?\nL78:   * Are there mechanisms in place to prevent abuses of power?\nL79:   * Do checks on the government’s power, such as a free and independent press, exist?\nL80:   * Is transition of power subject to the law?\nL81: \nL82: [Input]\nL83: ### Just Law\nL84: \nL85: Click to view Questions\nL86: \nL87: Just Law\nL88: Illustrative questions encompassed in this principle\nL89: \nL90:   * Is the law understandable to all people?\nL91:   * Is the law publicly available and easily accessible?\nL92:   * Is the law stable and steady?\nL93:   * Does the law apply equally to people of all backgrounds and social class?\nL94:   * Are core human, procedural, and property rights enshrined in the law?\nL95: \nL96: [Input]\nL97: ### Open Government\nL98: \nL99: Click to view Questions\nL100: \nL101: Open Government\nL102: Illustrative questions encompassed in this principle\nL103:   * Is it easy to find out how the government manages public resources and to request information from the government?\nL104:   * Can citizens collaborate with government officials to improve the rule of law?\nL105:   * Can people bring complaints to the government without facing repercussions?\nL106:   * Does the government respond to queries about its actions in a timely manner with useful information?\nL107:   * Are freedoms of opinion, expression, assembly, and association respected?\nL108: \nL109: [Input]\nL110: ### Accessible and\nL111: Impartial Justice\nL112: \nL113: Click to view Questions\nL114: \nL115: Accessible and Impartial Justice\nL116: Illustrative questions encompassed in this principle\nL117: \nL118:   * Is dispute resolution accessible and affordable?\nL119:   * Can people resolve their disputes in a timely manner?\nL120:   * Is the justice system free of corruption and improper influence?\nL121:   * Do the police, lawyers, and judges have adequate resources to administer justice?\nL122:   * Are the police, lawyers, and judges neutral and independent?\nL123: ## Benefits of effective rule of law\nL124: \nL125: No matter who we are or where we live, the rule of law affects us all. It is the foundation for communities of justice, opportunity, and peace—underpinning development, accountable government, and respect for fundamental rights.\nL126: Research shows that rule of law correlates to higher economic growth, greater peace, more education, and improved health outcomes.\nL127: \nL128: \nL129: \nL130:   * 【8†EconomyWhere rule of law is stronger, so is the economy.】\nL131:   * 【9†PeaceMore peaceful countries enjoy greater rule of law.】\nL132:   * 【10†EducationPeople are more educated where rule of law prevails.】\nL133:   * 【11†Life ExpectancyPeople live longer in countries with higher rule of law scores.】\nL134: \nL135: 【12†Image: GDP per capita 2020-2021】\nL136: \nL137: 【13†Image: Global Peace Index】\nL138: \nL139: 【14†Image: School life expectancy years】\nL140: \nL141: 【15†Image: Life Expectancy】\nL142: ## Over 8 billion people live in a country where rule of law is declining.\nL143: \nL144: How does your country score? 【16†Explore the Rule of Law Index】\nL145: \nL146: ### Take action\nL147: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhat is a ‘successful’ representative deliberative process for public decision making? Assessing the evidence: Innovative Citizen Participation and New Democratic Institutions | OECD (https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/innovative-citizen-participation-and-new-democratic-institutions_339306da-en/full-report/component-8.html)\nciteturn17view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/innovative-citizen-participation-and-new-democratic-institutions_339306da-en/full-report/component-8.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 4624\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL3: \nL4: 【2†OECD】\nL5: \nL6:   * [Button: Topics]\nL7:     1. Featured topics\nL8:     2. Agriculture and fisheries\nL9:     3. Climate change\nL10:     4. Development\nL11:     5. Digital\nL12:     6. Economy\nL13:     7. Education and skills\nL14:     8. Employment\nL15:     9. Environment\nL16:     10. Finance and investment\nL17:     11. Governance\nL18:     12. Health\nL19:     13. Industry, business and entrepreneurship\nL20:     14. Regional, rural and urban development\nL21:     15. Science, technology and innovation\nL22:     16. Society\nL23:     17. Taxation\nL24:     18. Trade\nL25:     19. Energy\nL26:     20. Nuclear energy\nL27:     21. Transport\nL28: #### Featured topics\nL29: \nL30: 【3†Image】\nL31: \nL32: 【4†Artificial intelligence】\nL33: \nL34: How to apply effective governance to harness the benefits of A.I. and mitigate its risks\nL35: \nL36: 【5†Image】\nL37: \nL38: 【6†Critical minerals】\nL39: \nL40: Critical minerals have become central to economic security as they are essential